{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793305899,"nonce":"8rJUVVR1r7Ers7z2sH4wUlRGOe4=","message":"Prioritized as item 3 of the WASM compilation-speed effort (2026-10-29), and the largest prospective benefit for repeated edits. Reuse this issue rather than creating a duplicate. Existing AIR reuse still regenerates LLVM IR and optimizes/emits every object.\n\nPlan stable, bounded compilation units and dependency-complete object keys. Cache lookup must happen before the expensive work it is intended to skip. Include imported inline bodies and relevant constants/layouts, signatures and symbol identities, compiler/LLVM/target/features/CPU/options/exception/fiber settings, native bindings and data/table registration conventions. Bytecode index renumbering and a small edit shifting instruction-balanced cuts must not silently reuse stale objects. Emit program data/registries fresh where appropriate; atomic publication, corruption fallback, eviction and concurrent-build behavior remain required.\n\nUse LLVM ThinLTO's summary/import/object-cache design as a reference, not a drop-in switch for Ash's custom linker. Measure unchanged and one-function-edit rebuilds against uncached and cold builds, with complete browser and CPU-sensitive checks, and record the actual units reused. Depends on settling stable shard ownership/import discovery; no speedup claimed yet.","files":null}]}